From sp_lev.c, for reference and for others:

1147    if (In_mines(&u.uz) && pm && your_race(pm) &&
1148          (Race_if(PM_DWARF) || Race_if(PM_GNOME)) && rn2(3))
1149        pm = (struct permonst *) 0;

(Line 1149 works out to "make a random monster" in context.)

So, if you are a dwarf or a gnome in the mines, and the level generator is 
about to make a monster of your race, then 2/3 of the time you are going to
get a completely random monster.

That means that of those 9 gnomes you list above, roughly 6 of them are 
going to be something else -- something hostile, most likely.  Any
non-dwarf 'h' that is generated (hobbits, or if you're really really
unlucky, mind flayers) will also be hostile, though I'm mostly thinking
of the hobbits here.

As a human, I will see all 9 gnomes.  I like this -- gnomes are crunchy!

I think we're more or less saying the same thing here, but interpreting
it in different ways.  I _like_ hostile gnomes.  They have single-digit
HP and die when you smack them with anything.  I'd rather not have the
increased chance of soldier ants, even with the built-in ranged weapon.  :/

> ~ future gnome and dwarf spawns in the mines are peaceful;

Future gnome and dwarf spawns in the mines are free XP for a human, and
dwarf spawns could be carrying mithril.  (Not that I'm against killing
peacefuls and looting the body, but it's nice not to _have_ to.)

> ~ having infravision;

I don't personally see much of the benefit from infravision, but then, I
was going for "racist" conduct on NAO, so I wouldn't have.

> ~ damage from crossbow bolts is d4+1/d6+1 instead of d6/d6 for arrows;

Monsters in the Mines are typically small, so 2-5 is effectively
equivalent to 1-6 here.  There's still the annoyance of being tempted to
increase crossbow skill and having it be useless later when the Longbow
appears and generates only arrows for you.

(As a side note to consider; orcish and elvish monsters often come with
arrows attached, making it much easier to both replenish arrows and get 
a free source of poison arrows.)

> ~ starting at 14 HP instead of 15;
> ~ gaining d6+1 instead of d6+d2;

Your HP gain changes midgame, also.  Once you reach a specific level (for a
Ranger, this is level 12), you will only be getting 1+0 extra HP as
opposed to 1+1, and gnomes don't get any Pw bonus over a human.  The
practical aspect of this is not likely to be terribly large, but sometimes
every HP matters, especially when you're getting so few per level; it looks 
like on average you'll be around 9HP short of where a human would be at XL14.

I just don't see enough advantages here from the gnome's infravision to
make up for the deficits in actual play. 

